<ThoughtPost
  title="Dabholkar on scientific thinking"
  date="2026-08-31"
  description="A talk by Dr. Narendra Dabholkar on scientific temper: lemons and chillies on Pune’s rickshaws, Sathya Sai Baba’s gold chains, an award he refused, and Galileo on his knees. Translated from Marathi."
>

<aside>
  <p class="aside-label">About this text</p>
  <p>What follows is not mine. It is a talk by Dr. Narendra Dabholkar (1945–2013), the doctor who gave up his practice to fight superstition and founded the Maharashtra Andhashraddha Nirmoolan Samiti. It was translated from Marathi with ChatGPT. The words are his; only the headings and layout are mine.</p>
</aside>

<p>I completed my MBBS in 1970. Until 1982, I ran two clinics and also managed a hospital. In 1982, I shut down both my clinics and closed the hospital as well. Since then, I have been a full-time worker of the Anti-Superstition Movement.</p>

<p>When I shut down my full-time, well-running medical practice and became a full-time activist, there was one question people asked me then—and still ask me even today: “Doctor, is it really necessary to shut down a successful business in order to work against superstition?” My answer is: <strong>yes, it is necessary.</strong></p>

<p>Why is it necessary? Understand this: if you light a candle in a dark hall, there is some light. If instead of a candle you light a petromax, there is more light. If instead of a petromax you light a tube-light, there is even more light. And if day breaks, the sun rises overhead, and sunlight enters from all directions, then darkness disappears completely. As light increases, darkness recedes.</p>

<p>Similarly, in human life, as the light of education and science spreads, ignorance and superstition should disappear. According to this way of thinking, nothing extra needs to be done. If this were truly the case, I would have been the happiest person. But what is the reality? I will give you just two examples so that the reality becomes clear.</p>

<h2>The train, the lemons and the chillies</h2>

<p>In 1853, India’s first railway train ran from Mumbai to Thane. Until then, people in this country had never imagined that a vehicle could carry hundreds of people from one place to another without animals like horses, elephants, or oxen. At that time, schoolchildren recited poems like:</p>

<blockquote><p>The sahib’s child is extremely clever,<br />How did he drive a cart without oxen?</p></blockquote>

<p>In those days, travelling from Thane to Mumbai by bullock cart took a full 24 hours. Compared to this, the railway was an extremely important and useful facility. At that time, there were no tickets for the train. In fact, to persuade people to board it, the British officers used to pay people one rupee to sit inside.</p>

<p>Why? Because people were not ready to get into the train. They believed the train was some kind of witchcraft. Fatwas were issued saying:</p>

<aside>
  <p class="aside-label">The fatwa</p>
  <p>“These white men will put you on the train, take you to Mumbai, where they have many construction sites, and crush you under the foundations to kill you. So people, do not board this train.”</p>
</aside>

<p>To encourage travel, one rupee was given to passengers—an amount that was significant at that time. Gradually, it was reduced to eight annas, then four annas. Later travel was made free, and eventually tickets were introduced.</p>

<p>But because the train was seen as something magical, whenever it departed—from Mumbai to Thane or from Thane to Mumbai—married women would come, perform rituals, apply turmeric and vermilion to the engine, and tie lemons, chillies, and inverted black dolls to it.</p>

<p>Whenever I narrate this, I see smiles on many faces in the audience. But honestly tell me—when a two-wheeler or a four-wheeler enters your home or your neighbour’s home today, don’t we do exactly the same thing?</p>

<blockquote><p>We accepted the products of science, but not the scientific mindset. We adopted scientific tools, but not scientific thinking.</p></blockquote>

<p>We once conducted a survey of twenty thousand auto-rickshaws in Pune. On every new moon day, eighteen thousand rickshaws had lemons, chillies, and black charms tied to them. Now, lemons contain vitamin C, which is extremely useful for nutrition—everyone knows this. This country has serious nutritional problems. Yet, every month, in Pune alone, we waste the vitamin C of eighteen thousand lemons.</p>

<p>Have we ever thought about this? We don’t wear clothes from 150 years ago. We don’t eat food from 150 years ago. We don’t follow education from 150 years ago. We don’t use vehicles from 150 years ago. Then why do we preserve lemons, chillies, and black dolls from 150 years ago?</p>

<p>Because we learned how to use science—but we never learned the scientific temper. But this issue is even more serious than it appears.</p>

<h2>Ash for us, gold for the Chief Minister</h2>

<p>Let me give you an example from the movement. Recently, Sathya Sai Baba passed away. Sathya Sai Baba used to say, “My miracle is my visiting card.” He claimed that the supreme controller of the universe had given him the power to perform miracles, so that people would believe that he was the great saviour performing miracles for their salvation.</p>

<p>If you visited Sathya Sai Baba, he would wave his hand in the air and place sacred ash in your palm; wave his hand and place gold or silver rings; wave his hand and give gold lockets or gem-studded necklaces—many such stories exist. But notice this carefully:</p>

<ul>
  <li>If an ordinary person like you or me visited, he would give <strong>ash</strong>.</li>
  <li>If an MLA or MP visited, he would give a <strong>gold or silver ring</strong>.</li>
  <li>If the President, Prime Minister, or a big industrialist visited, he would give a <strong>gold chain</strong>—to them or their daughters.</li>
</ul>

<p>Even Baba, it seems, gave according to the devotee’s status. Our status entitled us only to ash. Ash is just ash—but because it came from Baba’s “holy hands,” it was called <em>vibhuti</em>.</p>

<p>Now, Sathya Sai Baba was scheduled to visit Chakur in Latur district, where a massive temple was under construction during his lifetime. At that time, we were running an anti-superstition campaign in Latur district. I was speaking openly about these matters in public meetings and colleges.</p>

<p>The day Sathya Sai Baba arrived in Chakur happened to be the first day of the Maharashtra Legislative Assembly session. The Chief Minister, Vilasrao Deshmukh, was expected to attend the assembly—but instead, he came to Chakur for Baba’s darshan. There was an enormous crowd. Since we were planning protests, the police arrested all our activists from Latur, Beed, and Nanded districts 48 hours in advance.</p>

<p>The program began. Sathya Sai Baba started speaking. Five minutes later, he suddenly stopped. He waved his left hand in the air, produced a gold chain, and gave it to the Chief Minister’s younger brother, Diliprao Deshmukh (he still wears it today). He resumed speaking. After twenty minutes, he again stopped, waved his right hand, produced another gold chain, and gave it to Chief Minister Vilasrao Deshmukh himself (he too still wears it). At the end, he waved his hand again, produced ash, and distributed it among the gathered men and women.</p>

<p>Exactly what we had been saying—people saw it with their own eyes. I have written this incident in my book <em>Struggles Against Superstition</em>.</p>

<h2>The award I refused</h2>

<p>But the real irony comes later. One day, I received a letter from the Maharashtra government’s cultural department secretary, Patel. It said:</p>

<aside>
  <p class="aside-label">The letter</p>
  <p>“We are pleased to inform you that your book <em>Struggles Against Superstition</em> has been awarded the Krantisingh Nana Patil Award for Best Book, to be presented by Hon. Chief Minister Vilasrao Deshmukh. You are requested to come to Aurangabad on 25th November.”</p>
</aside>

<p>My reply to the Chief Minister is published in my book <em>Aise Kaise Zale Bhondu</em>. Among other things, I wrote one particularly serious line:</p>

<blockquote><p>I do not wish to accept an award from a Chief Minister who behaves in a manner inconsistent with the Indian Constitution.</p></blockquote>

<p>I am not an arrogant activist who rejects appreciation casually. Then why did I write this?</p>

<p>Because on 26 January 1950, when the Constitution came into force, it granted citizens rights—freedom of movement, freedom of expression, right to property. But just as parents later tell grown children that rights come with responsibilities, in 1976 the Constitution was amended to include Fundamental Duties. One of those duties is:</p>

<blockquote><p>It is the duty of every Indian citizen to promote scientific temper.</p></blockquote>

<p>It does not ask whether you want to do this or not. It declares it your duty. Later, in 1987, the new education policy included “development of scientific temper” as a core element. In Maharashtra, value education explicitly mentions nurturing scientific outlook.</p>

<h2>Scientific temper, in one word</h2>

<p>We live in the age of science. From morning till night, we cannot perform a single activity without science. A hundred years ago, Maharashtra had no toothbrush, toothpaste, or washbasin. People cleaned teeth with twigs and fetched water from wells.</p>

<p>Many of you are science graduates. After explaining all this in colleges, I ask one simple question: what is scientific temper, in one word? Ninety-nine percent of the time, the answer does not come—or comes wrong.</p>

<blockquote><p>Scientific temper, in one word, means causality—examining cause and effect.</p></blockquote>

<p>Every event has a cause. That cause can be understood by human intelligence. Even if all causes aren’t known yet, they will be known through causality. This is the most reliable path to true knowledge.</p>

<p>Before understanding causality, humans were helpless creatures. Everything was attributed to fate, destiny, karma, divine will. Once humans understood cause and effect, they became free. This is one of the most thrilling moments in human history.</p>

<h2>Why the human brain grew</h2>

<p>Consider this: humans arrived in this world only about five hundred thousand years ago. Our scientific name is “Man, the Homo sapiens,” or in full, “Homo sapiens sapiens.” <em>Homo</em> means animal and <em>sapiens</em> means wise. So how did such a physically weak animal become wise? Humans appeared five hundred thousand years ago. Our ancestors, the gorilla, orangutan, chimpanzee and the apes, appeared some fifteen million years ago. Mammals, whose females give birth to live young and feed them milk (elephants, tigers, lions), appeared around thirty-eight million years ago. The elephant came into being roughly forty million years ago and weighs 4,000 kilos. Humans came into being only five hundred thousand years ago and weigh about 60 kilos. So when I ask, “Relatively speaking, whose brain should be bigger?”, people pause. They know the human brain weighs about 1,350 to 1,500 grams, roughly a kilo and a half, in a 60-kilo body. That is one to one and a half percent of body weight. The 4,000-kilo elephant has a brain of just four kilos, one-tenth of one percent of its body weight.</p>

<p>So the real question before us is how the human brain became so much larger than the elephant’s. Suppose I told you that man is God’s favourite creature. He sat under a tree every day praying, “O God, give me sense, give me sense, give me sense,” and God, pleased, granted it, so his brain slowly grew. You would all laugh. And yet you revere Ganpati. You believe Gajanan is the god of intellect, and you know he has an elephant’s head. So why would the god of intellect, wearing an elephant’s head, give intelligence to humans and not to the elephant? What is the secret behind that?</p>

<p>Why did the human brain alone grow, and no other animal’s? The answer lies in evolution. Remember that man is an animal too, which is why we say “the human animal.” Like other animals, humans have paws. But the human thumb separated, the fingers became independent, and man began pressing his thumb against his fingers. He turned his ten fingers into a tool and began working on the nature around him. As he did, his brain kept growing. This took thousands of years. Then he picked up a stone from the ground and sharpened it. He fixed it to a branch broken from a tree and made the first primitive tool: the axe. That tool gradually improved. As his capacity to work on nature increased, his brain developed further. More thousands of years passed. Man discovered metal and made metal tools. With them his capacity improved still more, and his brain grew still more. Note this: it was through <em>labour</em>, by working on nature, that the human brain grew.</p>

<p>Man is not defined as “the thinking animal,” because all animals think. The definition is: <strong>Man is a tool-making animal.</strong> That is the first point. Here is the second. All animals, humans included, once walked on four legs, and humans later rose onto two. They turned their front legs into hands and used those hands to work on nature. When man stood upright, his voice box moved higher. The human vocal apparatus can produce more vibrations than any other animal’s, and so man developed language. On one side was a dialogue with nature through labour. On the other was a dialogue with other humans through language. That is how the human brain grew. It did not grow through worshipping any god, chanting, or performing sacrificial rituals.</p>

<p>So what happens when we fall prey to superstition? Narendra Dabholkar and the Andhashraddha Nirmoolan Samiti (the Committee for the Eradication of Superstition) lose nothing. Who loses, then? Suppose I visit your home and say, “What a lovely house! How did you manage to build such a big house so quickly?” You’d say, “Oh, I didn’t build it, my father did. My job is just to look after it.” Or suppose your mother’s friend sees her wearing a lot of jewellery and says, “So much gold! How did you manage to buy all this so young?” Your mother would say, “No, no, I didn’t buy these. My mother-in-law gave them to me.” Or she might say, “My mother gave them to me. This is my <em>stridhan</em> (a woman’s own inherited wealth), and it’s mine to safeguard.” You treasure a house or jewellery handed down by just one generation. This brain of yours, this treasure, took at least fifty thousand generations to come about. Skulls found in caves from five hundred thousand years ago have room for a brain of only 750 grams. Today your brain weighs 1,500 grams. When you forget that and fall for superstition, you give up your very title of “the wise human.” That is the first part.</p>

<h2>Galileo before the Church</h2>

<p>But remember, the full scientific name of the human being is <em>Homo sapiens sapiens</em>. That means not just “wise,” but a being that is becoming more and more wise. Then the question arises: when did this scientific temper—this quality that made humans wise—arrive in our country in the modern sense? When did it arrive in the world?</p>

<p>In reality, even in our own tradition—from the times of Charvaka and Buddha—a basic understanding of causality existed. But if we talk about the modern era and the modern meaning of scientific temper, it emerged roughly 400 to 500 years ago in Europe. This is what is known as the Copernican Revolution.</p>

<p>Copernicus, using mathematics, demonstrated for the first time that although it appears as if the sun revolves around the Earth, in reality it is the Earth that revolves around the sun. Aryabhata had stated this in India a thousand years before Copernicus, but we failed to preserve it, failed to advance it, failed to honour Aryabhata.</p>

<p>Nearly a thousand years after Aryabhata, Copernicus made the same claim. Initially, Europe paid no attention. But then Galileo emerged. In 1609, Galileo invented the telescope—a revolutionary invention for that time. Ships far out at sea appeared as if they were close. Ambassadors across nations informed their kings that this man had invented something extraordinary—enemy ships could be seen even from a distance.</p>

<p>Galileo was praised—but then he committed what proved to be a fatal “mistake.” He turned the telescope toward the sky. He began telling people, “People, come and see—”</p>

<ul>
  <li>The moon is not like a beautiful woman’s face; it has valleys and craters.</li>
  <li>The sun is not spotless; it has black spots.</li>
  <li>Venus has phases, just like the moon.</li>
  <li>And just as the moon is Earth’s satellite, Jupiter has three satellites of its own.</li>
</ul>

<p>I am certain that among those listening to me today, perhaps one—or very few—have actually seen Jupiter’s moons, the phases of Venus, the sunspots, or the craters on the moon. Galileo said all this 400 years ago. But Galileo was not praised for this.</p>

<p>The supreme authority of the Christian Church—the Pope—summoned Galileo before the Church tribunal. The Pope’s name was Urban VIII. He was Galileo’s friend. He was himself a scholar. When the order reached Galileo, he was ill. Galileo requested time, saying he would come once he recovered. But the Pope ordered that if Galileo could not walk, he should be chained and brought on horseback, because his crime was extremely serious.</p>

<p>Galileo was brought to the Vatican. A Church council was convened. Bishops and cardinals were seated. The Pope presided. Galileo was made to stand before them. Two charges were brought against him:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>“You are a liar.”</strong> Why a liar? Because “we see every day that the sun moves around the Earth, and you claim the Earth moves around the sun.”</li>
  <li><strong>“You are a heretic.”</strong> The second, and more serious, charge. A heretic—one who rejects true religious belief. And true belief was defined by what was written in the Bible. The Bible stated that the benevolent Father in heaven hung two lamps in the sky—one sun for daylight, one moon for night.</li>
</ol>

<p>Until the early 17th century, this was taught in Europe. Even though science had spread to some extent, Aristotle’s words were considered absolute authority. It was said: Aristotle was the master of all who know. Aristotle proposed two kinds of causes:</p>

<ul>
  <li>One was <em>efficient cause</em>—the search for cause and effect.</li>
  <li>The other was <em>final cause</em>—the will of the heavenly father.</li>
</ul>

<p>Galileo was told, “You are speaking against the Bible. You must apologise.”</p>

<p>After narrating this incident, I often ask audiences, “What do you think—did Galileo apologise or not?” Most people optimistically answer, “No, Galileo did not apologise.” But history tells us the truth. Galileo knelt down. He apologised unconditionally. He struck his own mouth and said:</p>

<blockquote><p>I was wrong. I will never again speak such foolish things. What you say is correct. The sun revolves around the Earth.</p></blockquote>

<p>Today, we teach first-grade students that although it appears the sun revolves around the Earth, in reality the Earth revolves around the sun. Then why did the man who proved this apologise?</p>

<p>Because 25 years earlier, a scientist named Giordano Bruno had been tied to a stake and burned alive for saying the same thing—and refusing to retract it. Galileo did not have that courage. Because he apologised, Galileo escaped execution but was sentenced to life imprisonment. During the final seven years of his life, he was blind and kept in solitary confinement.</p>

<p>Think about why this happened. Galileo was certainly speaking against the religious authorities. But look deeper and you’ll see he was offering the world a new account of cause and effect. Until then there was only one explanation for everything: the will of the Father in heaven. Galileo was saying, in effect: “Whatever the Father’s will may be, the Sun will appear to circle the Earth, the Earth will actually circle the Sun, and the Moon will move as it does. None of this depends on the Father’s will.” His discovery gave the world a new direction: investigating the reality around us through cause and effect. As a result, by grasping the scientific outlook, humanity has progressed more in the last 400 years than in the preceding 499,600.</p>

<h2>The bank of karma</h2>

<p>Does any of this concern our country? I think it concerns us very closely, because our religious philosophy contains the doctrine of <em>karmavipaka</em>, the ripening of karma. It says: “Whatever I suffer in this life is due to sins from my previous life, and I will reap the fruit of whatever good I do now in my next life.” This leaves me completely powerless over my own life. Every other bank in the world says, “Cash today, credit tomorrow.” The bank of karma says, “Credit today, cash tomorrow!” How strong is its spell on our minds even now? Think of the most popular song in <em>Geet Ramayan</em>, written by G. D. Madgulkar and sung by Sudhir Phadke:</p>

<!-- PASTE THE GEET RAMAYAN VERSE HERE, as:
<blockquote><p>line one<br />line two<br />…</p></blockquote>
-->

<p>In other words, nothing is in my hands. Whatever I endure is the play of my destiny, and that destiny was fixed by sins in a past life. I have no way of knowing what I did in my last life, and no way of knowing what I’ll get in the next for what I do now. Put plainly, the theory of karmavipaka runs directly against cause and effect.</p>

<p>It is the same even today. Suppose a son studies, passes with good marks and lands a good job at his very first interview. The family says, “The boy’s lucky, you know! Finished studying and got hired straight away!” Now suppose he studies and passes with good marks, the parents are even willing to pay to get him a job, and he runs around with recommendation letters from MLAs and MPs. If he still can’t find work for two or four years, the family says, “The boy’s just unlucky! We’re trying everything, but if it isn’t in his fate, what can we do?”</p>

<p>Similarly, suppose a daughter studies, passes with good marks and is introduced to a good family. She is liked at the very first meeting, and the wedding is settled at little cost. The family will say, “The girl’s fortunate! Didn’t give her parents a moment’s trouble.” Now suppose the opposite happens: the parents wear out their sandals for two or four years and no match works out. Then they’ll say, “Wretched girl has no marriage-luck at all! We’re doing everything we can, but in the end, marriage has to be written on your forehead, doesn’t it?”</p>

<p>Half in jest, I often ask college students this: “Ten of you are travelling in a jeep and it has a serious accident. Nine are injured and you alone escape. That’s your…” They answer in chorus, “Luck!” Then I say, “The jeep has a serious accident, all nine escape, and you alone are badly hurt. That’s also your…” Again they answer, “Luck!” Then I tell them jokingly: “I hope it never happens, but suppose you score very low in this year’s university exam. Your father scolds you: ‘We go through so much hardship for your education, and the professors work so hard to teach you. How did you get such low marks?’ You should reply: ‘Well, Father, it’s like this. Hardship was in <em>your</em> fate, hard work was in the professors’ fate, and these marks were in ours. Everyone has their own fate!’”</p>

<p>On the contrary, we must remember that nothing supernatural lies behind what happens in my life. There is no God, no destiny, no luck, no fate, no accumulated karma, no predestination, no karmic ripening, no sin or virtue from a past life, and no hour of my birth.</p>

<blockquote><p>Every event in my life has a cause. I can find that cause, and because I can find it, I can change it.</p></blockquote>

<h2>What Phule asked</h2>

<p>So when you forget the scientific outlook, you hand the reins of your life to someone else. When you embrace it, you take those reins into your own hands. Mahatma Phule said this a hundred and fifty years ago! He asked: “The Peshwa regime fought every battle only at an astrologically auspicious hour, and it collapsed. The white sahibs set out to battle without any auspicious hour, and they kept winning. Why?” He asked further: “Our mothers and sisters marry only after their horoscopes match on all 36 points, and still they are widowed young. White memsahibs marry without consulting any horoscope, and their marriages are happy. Why?” Notice that a hundred and fifty years ago, Phule was laying out the plain, practical causes behind both war and widowhood.</p>

<p>When two armies face each other, the outcome depends on their weapons, their morale and their numbers. It is not decided by the positions of the planets, stars, Moon, Sun or winds. If it were, every army would carry priests to consult the almanac and set the hour for battle. In Phule’s time, girls of six or seven were married to boys of ten. Savitribai was married at ten, and people were already mocking her as an “over-age bride.” Child mortality is still very high in Maharashtra, and back then it was enormous. Phule’s answer was: “Raise the age of marriage and stop child deaths. Then our mothers and sisters will not be widowed young, whether or not the 36 points of the horoscope match.”</p>

<h2>The horoscope that said someone would die</h2>

<p>That was said 150 years ago. Maharashtra today, believe me, is in a dire state! Almost every week I get a call from somewhere in the state. Sometimes it’s a young man, sometimes a young woman, sometimes the young man’s parents, and every time it’s the same story. If it’s a young man, he asks, “Is this Dabholkar’s number?” I say yes. “Is that you speaking?” Yes. “Do you have some time? I’d like to talk to you in detail.” I say, “Yes, I have time. That’s exactly my line of work. Go ahead.” He explains: “I’m an IT engineer. I’ve met a girl and I like her. She’s a doctor, and she likes me too. We want to marry. My family visited her home and met her household, her relatives and her wider family, and they’re happy with all of it. Then her family visited ours, met our household, relatives and family, and they’re happy too.”</p>

<p>After all that, I say, “So where do I come in? Go ahead and get married!” He says, “But Doctor, that’s exactly the problem! With everything nearly settled, one of our relatives needlessly showed our horoscopes to an astrologer. The astrologer says this marriage must not happen under any circumstances, because our charts don’t match at all. They’re so mismatched that they contain a <em>mrityu-shadashtak yoga</em>, a ‘death combination.’ He says that within six months of the wedding, either the boy’s father or the girl’s father will certainly die!”</p>

<p>If I’m speaking at a college, I ask the students what they would do if they were told this: marry or not? I also tell them: “Trust me and marry without a worry. Nothing will happen to your parents, and I’m willing to put that in writing on bond paper. It has been conclusively proven that predicting the events of a life from a horoscope has no basis whatsoever.” Refusing to let a horoscope decide whether I marry the girl I love is what it means to take the reins of my life into my own hands.</p>

<p>That is why the scientific outlook gives you a new kind of awareness. Do you know that famous couplet?</p>

<blockquote><p>ख़ुदी को कर बुलंद इतना<br />कि हर तक़दीर से पहले<br />ख़ुदा बंदे से ख़ुद पूछे<br />बता तेरी रज़ा क्या है</p></blockquote>

<p>It means: “Make your self-belief so strong that even if God himself writes every letter of your destiny, He too must write it as you wish.”</p>

<p>In short, the scientific outlook is a way of looking at life, of succeeding, and of becoming a better person. If we adopt it, it can bring radical change to our own lives, our communities, our society and our nation. My heartfelt appeal to you is to resolve to bring about that change!</p>

</ThoughtPost>
